@* Button's Overview, written by hand. Each section is a sentence and an example: a preview where
   it shows something the code doesn't, code alone where there's nothing new to see. Mode support,
   accessibility and the parameters follow the examples; the API page has the full reference. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A button is a control the user clicks to run an action. It suits posting a form, opening a page or running C#. For a button with only an icon, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/icon-button">IconButton</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Accents and variants</Heading>
<TextBlock>The accent sets a button's color, and the variant sets how strongly it stands out, from a solid fill to a link. Set <Code>Accent</Code> and <Code>Variant</Code>. Keep one solid primary button per area, for its main action.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="button-variants" />

<Heading Level="2">Submit and reset</Heading>
<TextBlock>A submit button posts its <Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor>, and a reset button resets its fields. Set <Code>Submit</Code> or <Code>Reset</Code> on a button inside the form. The browser does the work, so the form posts <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">with JavaScript off</Anchor> too.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="button-submit-reset" />

<Heading Level="2">Several submit buttons</Heading>
<TextBlock>A form can have several submit buttons, such as Publish and Save draft, and the server needs to know which was pressed. Give each one the same <Code>Name</Code> and its own <Code>Value</Code>; the form posts the value of the button pressed. <Anchor Href="demos/button/several-submit-buttons">See it run</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@SeveralSubmit</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Links</Heading>
<TextBlock>A button can be a link that looks like a button, for an action that opens a page. Set <Code>Href</Code> to render a link. To open it in a new tab, set <Code>External</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="button-links" />

<Heading Level="2">Sizes and icons</Heading>
<TextBlock>A button's size should match the fields beside it. Set <Code>Size</Code> to choose one. To show an icon before or after the label, set <Code>StartIcon</Code> or <Code>EndIcon</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="button-sizes-icons" />

<Heading Level="2">Loading and disabled</Heading>
<TextBlock>A loading button shows a spinner and blocks clicks while its action runs. Set <Code>Loading</Code> to show it. To turn the button off, set <Code>Disabled</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="button-states" />

<Heading Level="2">Running C# on click</Heading>
<TextBlock>On interactive pages, a button can run C# when it's clicked. Set <Code>OnClick</Code> to the method. On static pages, use <Code>Submit</Code> or <Code>Href</Code> instead.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="button-on-click" />

<Heading Level="2">Toggle buttons</Heading>
<TextBlock>A toggle button turns something on and off, such as a filter. A click toggles it, and screen readers hear whether it's pressed. On interactive pages, set <Code>@@bind-Pressed</Code>. Keep the label the same whether it's pressed or not. For toggles joined as one control, such as text styles, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/button-group#ex-button-group-toggles">ButtonGroup</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="button-toggle" />
<TextBlock>On static pages, set <Code>Pressed</Code> from the server's state, and post the change with <Code>Submit</Code>, <Code>Name</Code> and <Code>Value</Code>. A link can't be pressed.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>A loading button and a link that opens in a new tab have words for screen readers. To set them for the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>LoadingLabel</Code></td><td>What screen readers hear while <Code>Loading</Code> is set</td><td>"Loading"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>NewTabLabel</Code></td><td>What screen readers hear after an external link's text</td><td>"(opens in a new tab)"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<Heading Level="2">See also</Heading>
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/icon-button">IconButton</Anchor>: a square button with only an icon.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/button-group">ButtonGroup</Anchor>: related buttons joined into one control.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/toolbar">Toolbar</Anchor>: one row of controls; what doesn't fit moves into a … menu.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/confirm-button">ConfirmButton</Anchor>: asks before acting, with JavaScript off too.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>Demos: <Anchor Href="demos/button/several-submit-buttons">Several submit buttons</Anchor>, <Anchor Href="demos/button/toolbar">Editor toolbar</Anchor>.</ListItem>
    <TroubleshootingLinks Component="Button" />
</List>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        @* In a form: posts it, on any page. *@
        <Button Submit>Save</Button>

        @* A link that looks like a button. *@
        <Button Href="docs/get-started">Get started</Button>

        @* C# on click, on interactive pages. *@
        <Button OnClick="Refresh" Variant="ButtonVariant.Outline">Refresh</Button>
        """;

    private const string SeveralSubmit = """
        <Form Model="Post" FormName="post" OnValidSubmit="Save">
            ...
            <Button Submit Name="intent" Value="publish">Publish</Button>
            <Button Submit Name="intent" Value="draft" Variant="ButtonVariant.Outline">Save draft</Button>
        </Form>

        @code {
            // "publish" or "draft". Not "action": that name would hide the form's own.
            [SupplyParameterFromForm(FormName = "post", Name = "intent")]
            private string? Intent { get; set; }
        }
        """;
}
